# {{PLUGIN_NAME}} — {{ORG_NAME}}'s agent harness, as an opencode configuration

This directory *is* the configuration. It ships the same harness {{ORG_NAME}} runs
elsewhere — the verbs, the agent contracts, the gates — packaged for **opencode**.

> **Owned by {{ORG_NAME}}.** It is generated, but it is yours: identity, version and
> distribution are the org's, and nothing here is a runtime dependency on a generator.

## Install

**Globally** (every project on this machine):

```
cp -R ./ ~/.config/opencode/
```

**Or per repo** (checked in, so a team shares one harness):

```
mkdir -p <repo>/.opencode && cp -R ./ <repo>/.opencode/
```

Either way the layout is the same:

```
opencode.json    model + the one-provider allowlist + the subagent allowlist
AGENTS.md        session preamble (points at the wiki; run /{{VERB_PRIME}} first)
agent/           validate.md — the ONE validating agent: the read-only contract the native subagent tool derives from
command/         {{VERB_INTRO}} · {{VERB_SETUP}} · {{VERB_PRIME}} · {{VERB_INCEPTION}} · {{VERB_REFINE}} · {{VERB_EXECUTE}} · {{VERB_WIKI}} · {{VERB_HANDOFF}}
skill/           the procedure each command reads and follows
plugin/          dispatch.js — the writer grant + dispatch_read/dispatch_list (below); reminders.js — advisory nudges
```

**Restart opencode after copying.** Config, agents, commands and plugins are read at
startup — an already-running session will not see them.

## Upgrading

Re-run the generator over your existing org config and re-emit; the package is rebased
onto the newer generator. A config from an earlier generator version is **migrated, not
rejected**: the retired `opencode.subagents` cast block becomes the `opencode.nodes`
graph automatically (lossless, with a deprecation notice on stderr — delete the retired
block when convenient). A config that already declares `nodes` is untouched.

## Authenticate

No credentials live here. Log the provider in once with opencode itself:

```
opencode auth login      # pick {{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}
```

If `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` needs provider options (a region, a profile, a base URL), add
them under `provider.{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}.options` in your own `opencode.json` — that is an
opencode setting, not part of this harness. Default model: `{{OC_DEFAULT_MODEL_REF}}`.

## One provider

The load-bearing control is the **allowlist**: `opencode.json` sets

```
"enabled_providers": ["{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}"]
```

so `/models` lists **only** `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` — including on a machine that already
has an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or `OPENAI_API_KEY` in its environment, which opencode would
otherwise auto-detect as extra providers. `"disabled_providers": ["opencode"]` is kept
as belt-and-suspenders (it names the built-in Zen provider explicitly), but a deny-list
alone does **not** cover a provider you did not think to name — the allowlist does. The
`dispatch` tool enforces the same provider and the org's banned list per task.

## The read-only boundary — two engines, one contract

There is no fixed cast — one general agent carries a workflow, and the boundary attaches
to the **node**, not a persona. The node kind decides the engine:

- **Validate nodes (review, gate) → the native subagent tool** (`subagent_type:
  "validate"`). The child session gets `parentID` at create — native tree visibility the
  public API cannot give a dispatch child — and its permissions derive from the
  validating agent's own frontmatter (`agent/validate.md`): `{{OC_VALIDATE_DENY_LIST}}`
  denied, `bash` an allowlist of the tracker's and git's read commands. A denied
  permission removes the tool from the model's toolset *and* refuses at execution — so a
  validating run cannot edit code, run a mutating command, or spawn a writer to do it
  for it. The model is pinned in the frontmatter at definition time (the org floor) — a
  validating run never inherits the host default.
- **Produce nodes → `dispatch`** — the per-(repo, ticket) worktree grant. A native
  subagent child always runs in the parent's location (the subagent tool never passes a
  directory), so the one-worktree-per-(repo, ticket) invariant is genuinely dispatch's.

The deny set is **derived at emit time** from the graph's declared read surfaces
(`opencode.nodes` in the org config — deny = the dangerous capabilities the validating
nodes do not allow), so the config is what decides the artifact — not a hardcoded list
in a template. A validating node with no read surface, no fresh-context flag, or no
step cap does not emit.

**The spawnable set is allowlisted.** `opencode.json`'s `task` permission rule allows
exactly `{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}` (the primary agent) and `validate` under a `*: deny` — a
typo'd `subagent_type` is denied, never silently the full-permission primary agent, and
a hand-added agent file is not spawnable until the org config names it.

## Host compatibility

One artifact, two hosts: this configuration runs **unchanged on opencode 1.18.29+ and
on opencode 2.x**. `plugin/dispatch.js` and `plugin/reminders.js` carry both
entrypoints — 2.x loads `setup()`, 1.18.29+ loads `server()` — and both halves drive
the same node table, model policy and read-only boundary. The rest of the layout
(`agent/`, `command/`, `skill/`, `opencode.json`) is understood by both hosts; 2.x
translates the 1.x `permission` block automatically (`task` → `subagent`, `bash` →
`shell`). Host-specific mechanics are mapped, not duplicated — e.g. ad-hoc read-only
runs are enforced by tool gates on 1.x and by denied permission actions at session
create on 2.x.

Known differences on 2.x:

- `instructions` entries in `opencode.json` are accepted but not loaded by 2.x — it
  reads `AGENTS.md` instead, which this package ships with the same wiki pointers.
- The session-start toast nudge is 1.x-only for now: 2.x has no server-side toast
  API. The compaction handoff line (the load-bearing half) works on both hosts.

## `dispatch` — the writer grant, and ad-hoc delegations

The orchestrator (your session) hands produce work to one child run: `dispatch({ node,
ticket?, task?, repo?, model?, task_id?, command?, background? })`. Two modes, chosen by
what you pass:

- **Ticketed** (`ticket`, produce nodes only): the ticket is the whole envelope — the
  run reads it from the tracker, so the orchestrator never re-authors a prompt. The
  writer gets a worktree in `repo` under this workspace (so runs work across many repos
  at once) and may run in the `background` (its result is a tracker write). A ticketed
  **validate** dispatch is refused — validating nodes run through the native subagent
  tool (`subagent_type: "validate"`), which gives the child `parentID` at create and
  derives its read-only boundary from the validating agent's own frontmatter.
- **Ad-hoc** (`task`, no ticket): free-form, **ticketless, read-only** work — research,
  drafting, exploration that has no ticket yet. It never gets a worktree, and the write
  surface (`write`/`edit`/`patch`/`bash`) plus `task`/`dispatch` are denied on the prompt,
  so it cannot edit or commit whatever node it runs as. It **may** run in the background;
  its titled + summarized result is persisted to `.delegations/<task_id>.json` with a
  lifecycle (`running → complete | error`) that survives compaction, a restart, or a
  crash. Route shell-heavy read work through a native validate run instead.

Several calls in one turn run in parallel; `task_id` continues the same run (the feedback
loop); `model` picks the model for that task inside the org policy (one provider, banned
list). Ticketed runs are remembered in `.worktrees/.dispatch.json`, so a restart keeps
`task_id` continuation working. `dispatch` fails closed on an unknown node, a ticketed
validate run (route it to the native subagent tool), a call with neither a ticket nor a
task, an escaping repo, a banned/off-provider model, or a foreign `task_id`. When a
ticket is done, remove its tree:
`git -C <repo> worktree remove <workspace>/.worktrees/<repo>--<ticket>`.

**`dispatch_read({ task_id, timeout_ms? })`** blocks until an ad-hoc run reaches a
terminal state (or the timeout), then returns its persisted result — with a deterministic
status fallback if the result never persists. On completion a `<task-notification>` is
pushed into the parent session as a signal. **`dispatch_list()`** lists the store's runs
and their status. Both read only the on-disk store, so they work after compaction or a
restart.

Validating runs run bash only for the tracker's and git's read commands; opencode
matches the whole command text, so output redirection (`>`) and `--output` are denied
explicitly after the allows.
The `task` permission in `opencode.json` allowlists exactly the spawnable set, and the
validating agent denies `dispatch`, so a validating run cannot launder a write through
a child run.

## `plugin/reminders.js` — advisory, never blocking

Two nudges, both fail-open: a toast when a top-level session starts (run
`/{{VERB_PRIME}}`), and one line pushed into the compaction summary so a cycled session
knows the durable state lives in the tracker and `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`. Child run sessions
are not nudged.

